[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A write parses its own file now and defers the whole-graph rebuild

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SCRIBE-RECONCILE
TITLE: A write parses its own file now and defers the whole-graph rebuild
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Reconciliation has two tiers, and the split is what makes deferral safe.

Per write, immediately: re-parse the file just written, on its own. This costs
about 0.4 ms and catches a document that will not parse, naming the write that
caused it. It cannot see across documents.

Deferred to the next operation needing a whole-graph view: rebuild the graph
over the already-parsed documents. This is strictdoc's own build minus the file
walk -- about 20 ms against about 500 ms for the walk-and-unpickle it skips --
and it catches duplicate UIDs, dangling relation targets and missing required
fields. The repository's role-aware cycle check runs alongside it, because
strictdoc's own is inert on this corpus.

On failure the written bytes are restored AND the affected document is
re-parsed and swapped back, then the rebuild is re-run before anything is
served. Restoring bytes alone leaves a corrupt in-memory graph that a resident
process would keep answering from -- the difference between a daemon and a
one-shot command.

The builder exits the process on four failure paths and raises on others, so
both SystemExit and the exception have to be caught. The daemon never runs
under python -O, where the invariant asserts vanish.
<<<
